(1559078, 202992, 37252, '<td valign="top">** Greetings from the Critique Club **<br/> <br/>As stated in almost all other comments you received,including my own during voting, the image is sized too small for a fair evaluation. Size does matter here as larger reveals more detail.<br/>Composition is an equally important factor. While the image does follow pretty close to the rule of thirds, it is cropped too tightly on the left. Perhaps this was to crop out the passing boat, that is what I would assume as I can see some break in the water along the left that would not be from your own boat. <br/>Some comments you received mentioned the image appearing too dark. The water is perfect in my eye. Perhaps a little dodge tool on the skier herself would have made her stand out more.<br/>Nice action shot, keep up the great shooting.<br/> <br/>Linda</td>', '** Greetings from the Critique Club ** As stated in almost all other comments you received,including my own during voting, the image is sized too small for a fair evaluation. Size does matter here as larger reveals more detail.Composition is an equally important factor. While the image does follow pretty close to the rule of thirds, it is cropped too tightly on the left. Perhaps this was to crop out the passing boat, that is what I would assume as I can see some break in the water along the left that would not be from your own boat. Some comments you received mentioned the image appearing too dark. The water is perfect in my eye. Perhaps a little dodge tool on the skier herself would have made her stand out more.Nice action shot, keep up the great shooting. Linda', '2005-07-24 17:24:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(1559535, 202150, 39558, '<td valign="top">I think this is an awesome shot!!! I think it should have rated much higher! Good Job</td>', 'I think this is an awesome shot!!! I think it should have rated much higher! Good Job', '2005-07-24 22:31:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(1559550, 203115, 39558, '<td valign="top">I gave this a 7. I think it is a great shot at a shot. I think you should have been ranked so much higher..... Great photo!!!</td>', 'I gave this a 7. I think it is a great shot at a shot. I think you should have been ranked so much higher..... Great photo!!!', '2005-07-24 22:37:07.000000', NULL, 0),
(1559561, 202992, 39558, '<td valign="top">I scored it a 7. This photo should have been ranked much higher. I think I know how difficult it must have been being of guard. <br/>You captured it great!<br/>Sometimes I miss the whole subject when shooting boats and skiers. sometimes all I end up with is blurry water. Good Job!!!</td>', 'I scored it a 7. This photo should have been ranked much higher. I think I know how difficult it must have been being of guard. You captured it great!Sometimes I miss the whole subject when shooting boats and skiers. sometimes all I end up with is blurry water. Good Job!!!', '2005-07-24 22:44:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(1560422, 201166, 37252, '<td valign="top">** Greetings from the Critique Club **<br/> <br/>First of all, good action shot that fits the challenge theme.<br/>There are a few major problems though. First of all the colors are not very vivid - a boost in saturation can corerect this. Secondly, your white balance needed to be adjusted to compensate for the strong lighting you were under - namely direct overhead sunlight. Thirdly, the focus was off - leaving the image in it''s entirety a bit soft. <br/>I agree with some of yopur other commenters, the depth of field had it been more narrow would have blurred out the background people which are very distracting from the star of this shot.<br/>As for the composition, very nice. Your image fits into the rule of thirds, and in my eye is cropped just right - not to close or too wide.<br/> <br/>Linda</td>', '** Greetings from the Critique Club ** First of all, good action shot that fits the challenge theme.There are a few major problems though. First of all the colors are not very vivid - a boost in saturation can corerect this. Secondly, your white balance needed to be adjusted to compensate for the strong lighting you were under - namely direct overhead sunlight. Thirdly, the focus was off - leaving the image in it''s entirety a bit soft. I agree with some of yopur other commenters, the depth of field had it been more narrow would have blurred out the background people which are very distracting from the star of this shot.As for the composition, very nice. Your image fits into the rule of thirds, and in my eye is cropped just right - not to close or too wide. Linda', '2005-07-25 02:04:58.000000', NULL, 0),
(1560582, 202924, 37673, '<td valign="top">The foliage on the left has quite a few blow outs. One of the subjects is blown out. The sky is blown out. Focus is bad. Composition is fair. Would have lost the top of the photo so as to be just stairs. There is no relevance with the challenge. This is very odd because I have seen your work in  your portfolio a time or two and was very impressed. Not sure where you were going here but I know you have done MUCH better.  Looking forward to seeing more of your work.</td>', 'The foliage on the left has quite a few blow outs. One of the subjects is blown out. The sky is blown out. Focus is bad. Composition is fair. Would have lost the top of the photo so as to be just stairs. There is no relevance with the challenge. This is very odd because I have seen your work in  your portfolio a time or two and was very impressed. Not sure where you were going here but I know you have done MUCH better.  Looking forward to seeing more of your work.', '2005-07-25 02:55:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(1560669, 203027, 37673, '<td valign="top">The first thing that hits me is the big empty space on the right. The back ground if very over powering. Try less DOF. Rule of thirds would have helped this shot quite a bit. Had you composed the shot more to the left and given a bit of the defense it would have faired much better. The focus is suffering here also. It''s tough to catch a moving tatget like that but keep up the work. I sense the force is stong in this one...hahaha just a little lame Star Wars humor.</td>', 'The first thing that hits me is the big empty space on the right. The back ground if very over powering. Try less DOF. Rule of thirds would have helped this shot quite a bit. Had you composed the shot more to the left and given a bit of the defense it would have faired much better. The focus is suffering here also. It''s tough to catch a moving tatget like that but keep up the work. I sense the force is stong in this one...hahaha just a little lame Star Wars humor.', '2005-07-25 03:28:41.000000', NULL, 0),
(1560714, 202619, 32602, '<td valign="top">This is great. Look at his feet off the ground and the straight legs!<br/>Colour is really snappy and the reflection is cool too. Impressive!</td>', 'This is great. Look at his feet off the ground and the straight legs!Colour is really snappy and the reflection is cool too. Impressive!', '2005-07-25 04:01:06.000000', NULL, 0),
(1560875, 202457, 37608, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club,<br/> <br/> One of the first and most distracting things that I see in this image is the lighting; I know you dont have much control with this. The backs of the pants appear to be overexposed and the dark background really exaggerates this. Also the dark areas like the boy on the lefts glove and the runners uniform almost disappear into the wall. The focus seems to be a bit off as well. The color could be a little brighter the uniforms look faded and dull while the socks look over saturated.<br/> <br/> The way the two boys are looking off too the left leads you eyes out of the frame instead of leading into it. Just moving your angle to the right about 75 degrees would improve the photo dramatically, showing the boy about to touch the base while the ball was coming toward them giving it more suspense as well as showing all three faces. The dust is a good element but perhaps there is a little too much. <br/> <br/> Thank you for entering this in I hope everything I said helped you, feel free to PM me if you have any questions for me. <br/> <br/>                                             Nick  <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club,  One of the first and most distracting things that I see in this image is the lighting; I know you dont have much control with this. The backs of the pants appear to be overexposed and the dark background really exaggerates this. Also the dark areas like the boy on the lefts glove and the runners uniform almost disappear into the wall. The focus seems to be a bit off as well. The color could be a little brighter the uniforms look faded and dull while the socks look over saturated.  The way the two boys are looking off too the left leads you eyes out of the frame instead of leading into it. Just moving your angle to the right about 75 degrees would improve the photo dramatically, showing the boy about to touch the base while the ball was coming toward them giving it more suspense as well as showing all three faces. The dust is a good element but perhaps there is a little too much.   Thank you for entering this in I hope everything I said helped you, feel free to PM me if you have any questions for me.                                               Nick  ', '2005-07-25 07:24:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(1561444, 200795, 37608, '<td valign="top">:: Critique Club ::<br/> <br/> Well I guess I''ll start with the lighting, it is a little harsh causing dark shadows most noticeable under the chin. There is a lot of reflection off the person''s face. <br/> <br/> There doesnt appear to have much effort put into the photo, it looks like a snapshot when you where messing around with some buddys. Showing the object that hit him would have help as well as stepping back to capture more of your subject. Because you are so close his head seams to be disproportioned to his shoulders as well.<br/> <br/> The background doesnt help the photo either you lose the persons hair in it. The dots next to his mouth are distracting as well. Lighting would have helped this as well. If you were trying to convey boxing try hinting at it in the background too, Like maybe seeing people sitting behind red white and blue ropes.<br/> <br/> I hope this has helped feel free to PM me if you have any questions or would like more clarification on anything. Thank you.<br/> <br/>                                            Nick<br/></td>', ':: Critique Club ::  Well I guess I''ll start with the lighting, it is a little harsh causing dark shadows most noticeable under the chin. There is a lot of reflection off the person''s face.   There doesnt appear to have much effort put into the photo, it looks like a snapshot when you where messing around with some buddys. Showing the object that hit him would have help as well as stepping back to capture more of your subject. Because you are so close his head seams to be disproportioned to his shoulders as well.  The background doesnt help the photo either you lose the persons hair in it. The dots next to his mouth are distracting as well. Lighting would have helped this as well. If you were trying to convey boxing try hinting at it in the background too, Like maybe seeing people sitting behind red white and blue ropes.  I hope this has helped feel free to PM me if you have any questions or would like more clarification on anything. Thank you.                                             Nick', '2005-07-25 11:50:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(1561840, 201846, 37673, '<td valign="top">Very nicely done. A few minor flaws but overall you managed to capture a very impressive stunt. The rider could have been in focus and would have added to the shot. I know you couldn''t move the building in the background but, it is distracting. Maybe less DOF would have blurred it out. The composition is fine. Maybe if you had gotten closer and lower to the ground you could have captured him in nothing but sky. I shoot at a local skate park and I actually lie on the ground and shoot up. Although only with the riders I am familiar with.  Exposure seems fine also. Overall I really like the shot except the fore mentioned flaws. Great job. Keep up the good work.</td>', 'Very nicely done. A few minor flaws but overall you managed to capture a very impressive stunt. The rider could have been in focus and would have added to the shot. I know you couldn''t move the building in the background but, it is distracting. Maybe less DOF would have blurred it out. The composition is fine. Maybe if you had gotten closer and lower to the ground you could have captured him in nothing but sky. I shoot at a local skate park and I actually lie on the ground and shoot up. Although only with the riders I am familiar with.  Exposure seems fine also. Overall I really like the shot except the fore mentioned flaws. Great job. Keep up the good work.', '2005-07-25 14:58:10.000000', NULL, 0),
(1562423, 202913, 5792, '<td valign="top">Zoomer... Nice shot... you da man!</td>', 'Zoomer... Nice shot... you da man!', '2005-07-25 18:41:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(1562456, 200897, 22379, '<td valign="top">G''day from the critiques club :)<br/>I see a welcoming is in order - as this was your first entry on DPC, so welcome! This is my first comment as a CC, so we can learn together. DPC is a great learning tool for photographers, and there are a heapin'' helpin'' of wise and wonderful people in the forums always happy to help out - i hope you stick around for the ride.<br/> <br/>The first thing i noticed about this photo was the cool blue sky and changing leaves. The change must come early in CT. <br/>I can understand wanting to include this beautiful contrast in colours in the image, but i feel you needed to choose between colours or a soild silhouette. <br/>As the picture was taken, with the sun behind the tree, it looks more like a silhouette of  the tree (which is what i initially thought the photo was of, until i read the title) than of an archer.<br/> <br/>Sometimes the best of both worlds compete too strongly with each other.<br/>If you were to reshoot the image, please consider the following; <br/>A backlit image - choose a location in which the trees are still a little more full with leaves - this way you would have the rich blues and brown-orange colours  but not so much light is directly behind the archer that you lose all details, or that the outline blends into the outline of the trees.<br/>A true silhouette - Have the model move to an area where there is no distracting background at all, with the sun directly behind the model for a true silhouette.  Having a clear background is important in a silhouette, as the only details we have to understand the image are the outlines - so it is necessary for them to be strong and distinct.<br/> <br/>The slight upwards angle of the photographer to camera, is interesting, but i feel you could have used it more to your advantage, particularly as a silhouette. The upper body is the "important" part of a archers body, so exagerating it with a worms eye view (getting low to the ground and angling the camera up at the model) could compliment your theme. <br/>Right now, we see very little of the archer and a lot of extra "stuff" in the background - that does not add to the completed composition. I would have prefered to see more of the archers body, less of the scenery - a portrait orientation perhaps.   <br/> <br/>I hope you have found my comments helpful, please feel free to PM me if you have any questions - and WELCOME TO DPC :D<br/> <br/>Elli<br/></td>', 'G''day from the critiques club :)I see a welcoming is in order - as this was your first entry on DPC, so welcome! This is my first comment as a CC, so we can learn together. DPC is a great learning tool for photographers, and there are a heapin'' helpin'' of wise and wonderful people in the forums always happy to help out - i hope you stick around for the ride. The first thing i noticed about this photo was the cool blue sky and changing leaves. The change must come early in CT. I can understand wanting to include this beautiful contrast in colours in the image, but i feel you needed to choose between colours or a soild silhouette. As the picture was taken, with the sun behind the tree, it looks more like a silhouette of  the tree (which is what i initially thought the photo was of, until i read the title) than of an archer. Sometimes the best of both worlds compete too strongly with each other.If you were to reshoot the image, please consider the following; A backlit image - choose a location in which the trees are still a little more full with leaves - this way you would have the rich blues and brown-orange colours  but not so much light is directly behind the archer that you lose all details, or that the outline blends into the outline of the trees.A true silhouette - Have the model move to an area where there is no distracting background at all, with the sun directly behind the model for a true silhouette.  Having a clear background is important in a silhouette, as the only details we have to understand the image are the outlines - so it is necessary for them to be strong and distinct. The slight upwards angle of the photographer to camera, is interesting, but i feel you could have used it more to your advantage, particularly as a silhouette. The upper body is the "important" part of a archers body, so exagerating it with a worms eye view (getting low to the ground and angling the camera up at the model) could compliment your theme. Right now, we see very little of the archer and a lot of extra "stuff" in the background - that does not add to the completed composition. I would have prefered to see more of the archers body, less of the scenery - a portrait orientation perhaps.    I hope you have found my comments helpful, please feel free to PM me if you have any questions - and WELCOME TO DPC :D Elli', '2005-07-25 18:58:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(1562501, 202752, 37673, '<td valign="top">The reflection of the foliage in the water is very nice. Unfortunately That''s it. The fisherman is out of focus. The 3 branches in the foreground are distracting. There is a lot of un-remarkable area on the right. It looks as though some of the rocks are blown out or just about to be. His vest, shirt and cap seem to be a bit over exposed. The colors are very nice as is the strings contrast with the picture. <br/> <br/>This is OT but I would like to tell you the photo in your portfolio "too late to play" is amazing.</td>', 'The reflection of the foliage in the water is very nice. Unfortunately That''s it. The fisherman is out of focus. The 3 branches in the foreground are distracting. There is a lot of un-remarkable area on the right. It looks as though some of the rocks are blown out or just about to be. His vest, shirt and cap seem to be a bit over exposed. The colors are very nice as is the strings contrast with the picture.  This is OT but I would like to tell you the photo in your portfolio "too late to play" is amazing.', '2005-07-25 19:25:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(1562502, 202350, 37252, '<td valign="top">*** Welcome from the Critique Club ***<br/> <br/>What a wonderful photo! Great action shot and shows creativity. Anyone can take a picture of a ball being tossed, or a bat being swung, but you went beyond that and captured the emotion of a "pickle". The clarity of all the players faces and actions are well demonstrated. Colors are vivid, with all areas looking properly exposed. I really like the composition. You have all the involved players, both directly and indirectly in the shot - and kept the image as a whole very well balanced.<br/>Keep up the good shots!<br/> <br/>Linda</td>', '*** Welcome from the Critique Club *** What a wonderful photo! Great action shot and shows creativity. Anyone can take a picture of a ball being tossed, or a bat being swung, but you went beyond that and captured the emotion of a "pickle". The clarity of all the players faces and actions are well demonstrated. Colors are vivid, with all areas looking properly exposed. I really like the composition. You have all the involved players, both directly and indirectly in the shot - and kept the image as a whole very well balanced.Keep up the good shots! Linda', '2005-07-25 19:26:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(1562521, 203062, 40647, '<td valign="top">Great action shot!, love the saturation to make the players stand out agains the background, is artistic!   Coooool!!9.5<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-07-27 18:25:16.</i></td>', 'Great action shot!, love the saturation to make the players stand out agains the background, is artistic!   Coooool!!9.5', '2005-07-25 19:45:44.000000', '2005-07-27 19:25:16.000000', 0),
(1562762, 202681, 22379, '<td valign="top">G''day from the Critique Club Martin.<br/> <br/>First off, let me just say i "get" the photo, and the point you were making (and come on its a sports watch people!). DPC voters are not too pro thinking-outside-the-box. By no means should you quit thinking outside the box, but it is important to recognise that even the most techically perfect shot will not recieve the justice (in score) it deserves if it is a little left of mainstream thinking. Some people at DPC shoot for the voters, others (like me) are happy to be voted down and keep the creativity and originality flowing - which path you choose to take depends on what you want to "get out of" DPC. Neither is right or wrong. You have to be a little thicker skinned for the latter though ;)<br/> <br/>I would like to say more about the aperture - but i like to know the EXIF information to do so. If you don''t know how to get at your EXIF information, consider downloading Picasa, it is a small, and free image cataloguing program by google, you can click properties on the image in question and it will tell you the cameras aperture and shutter speed (among other things). This feature is probably available in whatever software you are using right now, if you have a look.<br/> <br/>Looking at the image however, i would guess at about a f2.8 . Personally, i would have  used something closer to a 6 or 7 so that the entire face of the watch and the weights were in focus. I could not tell that that was the bar of a dumbell until i read what it was in a comment - it is too out of focus for a weight-lifting ignorant to tell what it is, but close enough to the focal DOF that you sit there saying what the heck is that thing. At this aperture i would have preferd it not there at all, because i spent too much time wondering what it was and why you had included it in the composition. <br/> <br/>I enjoy the lighting, and the composition is fairly soild (though would have ''made more sense'' with a different aperture setting). <br/>The post processing is perfect for this image, and i do believe it deserved higher than a 4.0 . <br/> <br/>If you have an comments or questions, feel fre to PM me :)<br/>Elli<br/></td>', 'G''day from the Critique Club Martin. First off, let me just say i "get" the photo, and the point you were making (and come on its a sports watch people!). DPC voters are not too pro thinking-outside-the-box. By no means should you quit thinking outside the box, but it is important to recognise that even the most techically perfect shot will not recieve the justice (in score) it deserves if it is a little left of mainstream thinking. Some people at DPC shoot for the voters, others (like me) are happy to be voted down and keep the creativity and originality flowing - which path you choose to take depends on what you want to "get out of" DPC. Neither is right or wrong. You have to be a little thicker skinned for the latter though ;) I would like to say more about the aperture - but i like to know the EXIF information to do so. If you don''t know how to get at your EXIF information, consider downloading Picasa, it is a small, and free image cataloguing program by google, you can click properties on the image in question and it will tell you the cameras aperture and shutter speed (among other things). This feature is probably available in whatever software you are using right now, if you have a look. Looking at the image however, i would guess at about a f2.8 . Personally, i would have  used something closer to a 6 or 7 so that the entire face of the watch and the weights were in focus. I could not tell that that was the bar of a dumbell until i read what it was in a comment - it is too out of focus for a weight-lifting ignorant to tell what it is, but close enough to the focal DOF that you sit there saying what the heck is that thing. At this aperture i would have preferd it not there at all, because i spent too much time wondering what it was and why you had included it in the composition.  I enjoy the lighting, and the composition is fairly soild (though would have ''made more sense'' with a different aperture setting). The post processing is perfect for this image, and i do believe it deserved higher than a 4.0 .  If you have an comments or questions, feel fre to PM me :)Elli', '2005-07-25 21:34:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(1562990, 202739, 19708, '<td valign="top">Unbelievable timing.<br/>I am especially impressed at how you setup the exposure to make the shot.<br/>Congrats on your second ribbon Larus.</td>', 'Unbelievable timing.I am especially impressed at how you setup the exposure to make the shot.Congrats on your second ribbon Larus.', '2005-07-25 23:18:04.000000', NULL, 0),
